s=Slide('Walk one request: reading a signed-in profile','How the request, identity and data move across our layers','src/api/accounts.ts; hooks/useAccount.ts; server/api.mjs; Register.cs; AppAuthJson.cs','Use a profile read to explain the consequential design. Browser fetch sends GET /api/profile with an opaque HttpOnly cookie, no body token. Node looks up the 12-hour local session and selects the stored backend user token. The forwarding client supplies app_access, verified IP and a cached login-issued service admin_token. Node http(s).request can send the backend GET JSON body; browser fetch cannot. AppAuthJson validates app/IP and visitor token; Register reads user/school and returns profile data. Node projects the four permitted public fields. No password, user token or service token reaches the browser.',takeaway='GET bodies are an upstream contract constraint; the browser-side contract remains a normal same-origin request.')
steps=[('1 · Browser','GET /api/profile + opaque cookie; no credentials in localStorage.'),('2 · Node adapter','Cookie ID → session Map → visitor token; add server-owned envelope.'),('3 · ASP.NET + SQLite','Validate app/IP/token; select user and school; produce profile data.'),('4 · Browser-safe result','Return email, first_name, last_name, school; expire true invalid sessions.')]
