s=Slide('Three identities and two expiry clocks','Browser session, visitor token and service identity serve different purposes','api.mjs sessions/forwarding client; AppAuthJson.ValidateApp/ValidateToken; Env.cs','Keep these three identities separate. se_session is an opaque cookie ID. Node maps it to the visitor access_token. Separately, a configured service account logs in and its admin access token becomes admin_token for trusted IP forwarding. Human admin authorization comes from the visitor’s own token and Admins level; it is not conferred by the service token. to_user is a selector for an eligible target while sudo_id/caller level retain provenance. Local Map expiry is 12 hours; backend ordinary/admin tokens last 21/3 days. Public school reads and login admission have different checks from token-authenticated actions.',takeaway='Service privilege transports identity; it does not turn an ordinary visitor into a human administrator.')
s.table(['Data item','Held / used where','Meaning'],[['se_session','Browser cookie ↔ Node Map','Random opaque ID; fixed 12-hour local session.'],['access_token','Node memory → backend JSON','Visitor or human-admin identity; backend 21/3-day lifetime.'],['admin_token','Node service cache → backend envelope','Login-issued service-admin proof for IP forwarding.'],['to_user + sudo_id','Backend selected-user context','Target selector plus preserved original caller provenance.']],[330,500,640],rowh=93,size=21)
s.rect(65,674,1470,62,GOLD,None,5);s.text(85,692,'Restart / scaling',22,INK,True);s.text(380,692,'Node restart loses local sessions; independent replicas need shared session state.',21)

s=Slide('Technology decisions: constraints, benefits and trade-offs','Protocol compatibility, client state and single-instance storage shape the design','package.json; vite.config.ts; api.mjs; csproj; Sql.cs; render.yaml','Use precise reasons, not generic technology praise. React/TypeScript fits a stateful UI and explicit browser contracts. Node is consequential because browser fetch cannot send the upstream GET JSON body and shared credentials should stay off the client. ASP.NET supplies typed controller/validation infrastructure already present. SQLite fits a portable single-instance prototype but brings path, schema migration and concurrency/foreign-key obligations. Render hosting uses the existing Node manifest; Azure is the configured API destination. These explanations are inferred design fit, not a claim that a formal ADR or original team rationale exists.',takeaway='The Node adapter is the most consequential compatibility decision; local state and SQLite also impose explicit operating limits.')
s.table(['Choice','Why it fits this implementation','Trade-off / consequence'],[['React 19 + TypeScript + Vite','Interactive hooks/forms; typed client contracts; fast production bundle.','Local state is not persistence; accessibility can regress with JSX changes.'],['Node.js 24 adapter','Supports upstream GET bodies; retains credentials; one browser origin.','Extra network hop; in-memory sessions; service identity lifecycle.'],['C# / ASP.NET Core · .NET 9','Typed controllers and existing account/admin validation paths.','Body-credential contract and static-helper coupling need deliberate handling.'],['SQLite + parameterized SQL','Portable relational prototype with account/school/security relationships.','Versioned schema, one coherent path and verified FK/concurrency behavior.'],['Render + Azure configuration','Static UI/API adapter separate from backend deployment.','Configuration/health alone does not prove cross-service readiness.']],[370,560,540],rowh=91,size=19)

s=Slide('Deployed: Azure backend + Render website','Team-confirmed hosting · https://studentexchange.onrender.com · workflow acceptance remains separate','render.yaml; server/index.mjs, app.mjs; Env.cs; Website/docs/live-validation.md','Render manifest specifies Node 24.19.0, npm ci/build, npm start, /healthz and one instance. Server-only backend/app_access/user/user_pass settings must be configured; the service user must actually be an admin under the deployed backend contract. The configured Azure URL identifies a destination, not proof of readiness. The team confirms Azure backend and Render website deployment. Historical October 8/9 live workflows preceded the October 10 service-token migration; those dated notes do not independently verify the currently deployed authentication contract. Account DB schema/config files and key pairing must exist. Rollback should preserve compatible configuration/schema. Do not display credentials or run privileged live mutations during this review.',takeaway='/healthz reports process/configuration presence; it does not prove service login, database readiness or marketplace integration.')
